WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.512
Hi, I'm Wombat!

00:00:02.512 --> 00:00:04.955
The Z1 chip doesn't multiply matrices.

00:00:04.955 --> 00:00:06.584
It computes by sampling.

00:00:06.584 --> 00:00:09.842
Here's how Z1T fits a transformer onto it.

00:00:11.392 --> 00:00:13.909
Z1 computes by sampling, not multiplying.

00:00:13.909 --> 00:00:18.942
Z1T keeps a transformer's block shape and rebuilds every primitive inside it

00:00:18.942 --> 00:00:21.458
around 16 fixed neighbours per bit.

00:00:23.275 --> 00:00:28.245
Z1 is a fabric of pbits: random bits, each wired to sixteen fixed neighbours.

00:00:28.665 --> 00:00:30.544
Weight the neighbours' states by their couplings.

00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:36.736
The average is a tanh: the nonlinearity a network wants, sampled, not computed.

00:00:37.156 --> 00:00:41.173
One draw is one bit, so reading out a number means averaging many draws.

00:00:43.025 --> 00:00:48.497
A standard block assumes dense reach: a norm, dense feed-forward layers, softmax attention.

00:00:48.917 --> 00:00:53.534
Z1T swaps the norm for a tanh, the feed-forward for sparse tanh-linear

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:57.382
units, and attention for a gated convolution with no softmax.

00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:03.003
Z1 can't do embeddings or positions efficiently, so those run on an

00:01:03.003 --> 00:01:04.685
FPGA on the same board.

00:01:05.105 --> 00:01:09.381
The sparse tanh-linear layers and attention gates run on Z1, as sampling.

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:13.231
Residual adds go back to the FPGA, block after block.

00:01:13.651 --> 00:01:15.838
The FPGA also projects to logits.

00:01:15.838 --> 00:01:20.213
In this first version, it spends over ninety-five percent of the energy.

00:01:21.592 --> 00:01:25.721
The over a hundred times claim is one row: an H100

00:01:25.721 --> 00:01:27.222
at ten percent utilization.

00:01:27.222 --> 00:01:30.975
At fifty and a hundred percent, it's twenty-eight and fourteen.

00:01:30.975 --> 00:01:31.726
All projected.

00:01:33.517 --> 00:01:38.083
Z1T keeps a transformer's shape and rebuilds its layers so sixteen fixed

00:01:38.083 --> 00:01:41.507
wires can sample them; the FPGA does the rest.

00:01:43.025 --> 00:01:46.643
To recap: a sampling chip, a transformer rebuilt to fit it, and

00:01:46.643 --> 00:01:48.150
an FPGA for the rest.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.662
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:50.662 --> 00:01:51.260
I'm Wombat.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:51.559
Bye!
